					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/aceofmoxes" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2943332); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2943332&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608498400" alt="Aceofmoxes" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2943332)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/aceofmoxes" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2943332); return false;" >Aceofmoxes</a></span> <span class="odate time_1491239595 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">03 Apr 2017 17:13</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2782801">
									

<p>The lottery problem is well-done here.</p>
<p>However, one contention,</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The in­ter­me­di­ate value the­o­rem from cal­cu­lus guar­an­tees that at there is at least one num­ber that, if trans­mit­ted, would end up be­ing cor­rect, but there is no way this num­ber can be de­ter­mined be­fore­hand.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The in­ter­me­di­ate value the­o­rem only applies over <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">continuous functions</span> Darboux functions<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-48575-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-48575-1')">1</a></sup>. Lottery numbers are discrete, huge, but limited. So for instance, a simple situation with a coin flip lottery.</p>
<p>Sending Heads changes the winner to Tails.<br />
Sending Tails changes the winner to Heads.</p>
<p>And there is no winning message.</p>
<p>If I'm following you correctly, then, it would be much easier to just say that each time travel event re-randomizes<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-48575-2" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-48575-2')">2</a></sup> sensitive events. Then the odds of transmitting back a winning ticket are the same as guessing a winning number at first.</p>
<p>The better way to win the lottery is to send back every losing ticket, buy one more, and send that one back, too. Eventually, your growing pile of tickets will include the winning number.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-48575-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-48575-1')">1</a>. <a href="https://www.encyclopediaofmath.org/index.php/Darboux_property">https://www.encyclopediaofmath.org/index.php/Darboux_property</a><br />
Still doesn't apply to lottery numbers.</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-48575-2"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-48575-2')">2</a>. Imprecise language</div>
</div>

							</div>

								
