<blockquote>
<p>The same message would always create the same change, but that's impossible to predict from your reference point, and it's impossible to return to your reference point once you try it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>With one caveat: there's increasing experimental evidence from those fun little &quot;hacking the universe&quot; quantum physics experiments that <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bell%27s_theorem">Bell's Theorem</a> was right, and the Bell inequalities have as many holes as a sieve. This has a huge number of implications, but the most important one here is that even if you could go back to your reference point and send the same message back in time twice, it wouldn't have <em>exactly</em> the same outcome- only one with a high likelyhood of being very similar.</p>
<p>In the purest sense with the coin flip example, you'd be letting the coin be flipped again, only this time you have the added knowledge of macro-scale conditions which influenced it to land on heads, provided you didn't change those conditions by telling yourself it was more likely to land on heads. Do it enough times, and you could eventually figure out a message that didn't cause you to do something that made it land on tails, or its edge, or roll under the couch, or&#8230;</p>

							</div>

								
