<p>You are right of course, but strictly speaking it <em>isn't</em> possible to ever send exactly the same message twice or ever really return to exactly the same reference point (or <em>thread</em>) anyway. World lines are really just a way of describing bounded regions of thread space that are 'close enough' to each other to be considered the same for a given analysis, allowing us to describe the asymptotic behavior without having to worry about the details. There are cases where this description of time travel breaks down, in the case of aperiodic and chaotic systems (chapter 4). Really though, I just need to finish chapter 3, it will explain this in all its analytical glory.</p>

