			<div class="content" id="post-content-2783649">
									

<blockquote>
<p>I'm still a little unsure about the need to also send the information necessary to win. You'd need to include the winning lottery number in any message</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not necessarily, the message could be anything at all; all we need is for whatever lottery number we pick and enter with to match the lottery drawing. Given a finite set of lottery numbers <span class="math-inline">$L$</span>, and a recursively enumerable message space <span class="math-inline">$M$</span>, then <em>any</em> computable function <span class="math-inline">$f: M\to L$</span> would work to eventually give us the correct lottery number.</p>
<p>For instance, you could send successive natural numbers and compute its modulus with respect to the set of lottery numbers. Or you could send galleries of cat pictures, adding a new picture each time, and take a cryptographic hash of the collection to determine the lottery number. You could send lists of jokes and just pick &quot;0000000000&quot; every time. Or you could send meaningless binary blobs, and use the same decision process the lottery itself uses to pick the number. It doesn't really matter, all these cases are isomorphic.</p>

							</div>

								
