					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1918720&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608498400" alt="Scorpion451" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1918720)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;" >Scorpion451</a></span> <span class="odate time_1491355828 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">05 Apr 2017 01:30</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2783850">
									

<p>To put it another way, that might work for the specific aspect of winning the lottery if you don't set off some sort of butterfly effect, and that Bell's theorem thing doesn't make the third ball a 34 instead of a 5 just because.</p>
<p>You're still going to change <em>something</em> by virtue of altering the past, though- say, someone who was &quot;supposed&quot; to win doesn't because you threw off the RNG in the lottery machine by tampering with it from the future.</p>

							</div>

								
					
